            "The <strong>fluid balance chart</strong> records every intake — intravenous fluid by the burette level, feeds by volume (a breastfeed is recorded by time and estimated, or by test-weighing before and after on the same scale: 1 g gained = 1 mL taken), oral medicines and flushes — and every output: <strong>a wet nappy is weighed on the same electronic scale as a dry one of the same type, and the difference in grams is the urine in millilitres</strong>; stool and vomit are estimated or weighed; a catheter is read hourly in the acutely ill child; drains and nasogastric aspirates are measured. The targets: <strong>urine output at least 1 mL/kg/h in a child and 2 mL/kg/h in an infant</strong> (0.5 mL/kg/h in an adolescent) — a 10 kg child should produce 240 mL a day, 10 mL an hour; fewer than four wet nappies in 24 hours in an infant is oliguria. Totals are struck every four hours and at 24 hours, and the difference is compared with the change in weight — if the chart says +400 mL and the scale says −300 g, the chart has missed the losses.",
            "<strong>Fluid overload</strong> is the complication the nurse must see first because the child will not report it: a <strong>rising respiratory rate</strong> and new effort, <strong>crackles</strong>, a <strong>gallop rhythm</strong>, <strong>puffy eyelids</strong> and sacral or scrotal oedema, an <strong>enlarging liver</strong> edge, weight gain, falling saturations, frothy secretions and restlessness. The response is to <strong>stop the infusion, sit the child up, give oxygen and call</strong>; furosemide 1 mg/kg IV is given on instruction. The children at risk at ordinary volumes are the <strong>severely malnourished, the severely anaemic, those with pneumonia, bronchiolitis, meningitis, heart disease or renal failure</strong>, and the newborn — in whom 'maintenance' from the chart is often too much, and in whom the chart, the scale and the stethoscope are reviewed together at every handover.",
