            "Write the <strong>weight</strong> on every fluid and feed prescription and recalculate whenever it changes by 10 per cent. Prescribe and record in <strong>millilitres per hour</strong> and, for a drip without a pump, <strong>drops per minute</strong>, checked by counting against a watch for a full minute at the start, after every adjustment and hourly. Use the <strong>burette</strong> with one to two hours of fluid and the <strong>pump</strong> for every infusion that contains potassium, a drug or is going into a baby. Label every bag and burette with the additives, the time and the rate. Check the <strong>cannula site hourly</strong> and at every drug — a swollen, blanched or leaking site is stopped and resited before the next dose. Keep the <strong>chart live</strong> — recorded as it happens, totalled four-hourly, compared with the weight daily. Watch the <strong>respiratory rate</strong> as the earliest sign of overload and the <strong>capillary refill and urine output</strong> as the signs of too little. Hand over the fluid plan, the balance and the next review time by name at every shift change. And whenever a calculation surprises you — a rate above 200 mL/h in a toddler, a feed above 50 mL for a 1.5 kg baby, a bolus into a child with oedema — <strong>stop and recalculate with a colleague</strong>; the surprise is usually right.",
